<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">France Antarctique</h1><p class="st-meta">1555 CE – 1567 CE · capital Fort Coligny</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived French colony in Guanabara Bay whose destruction by the Portuguese led directly to the founding of Rio de Janeiro.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1555 the French admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</a>, backed by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-coligny">Gaspard de Coligny</a>, sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> with some 600 settlers. They built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-coligny">Fort Coligny</a> on a small island and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a>, who traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a> with French merchants.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was intended partly as a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">French Protestants</a>, and a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinists</a> sent from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> arrived in 1557, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-de-lery">Jean de Léry</a>, whose account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> became a classic of travel writing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon">Villegaignon</a>, however, quarrelled with them over doctrine and expelled them, and some were executed. Harsh discipline and desertions weakened the settlement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-coligny">Fort Coligny</a> in 1560. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estacio-de-sa">Estácio de Sá</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1565 and, with Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temimino">Temiminó</a> forces, drove the remaining French and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> allies from the bay by 1567.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/france-antarctique.jpg" alt="France Antarctique" width="320" height="236"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown or Pierre Duval · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LA_FRANCE_ANTARCTIQVE_autrement_LE_RIO_IANEIRO_Tir%C3%A9e_des_Voyages_que_Villegagnon,_et_Jean_de_Leri_ont_faits_au_BRESIL_Les_Ann%C3%A9es_1557._et_1558.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1555 CE – 1567 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Coligny</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Antarctic France, France Antarctique (Rio de Janeiro)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1561&amp;focus=france-antarctique" aria-label="France Antarctique on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3527 3023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where France Antarctique lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3847 3279L3845 3279M3847 3279L5054 728M3847 3279L5054 728M3847 3279L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3845 3279h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3847 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3847 3279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1561&amp;focus=france-antarctique">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
